      "content": "<h3>What is a Vector?</h3>\n<p>A <strong>Vector</strong> is a resizable array, just like <code>ArrayList</code>, with one big difference: <strong>every method is synchronized</strong>, so only one thread at a time can use it. It is one of the <strong>oldest</strong> classes of Java (from version 1.0, before the Collection Framework existed) and was later retrofitted to implement <code>List</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Package <code>java.util</code>. Implements <code>List</code>, <code>RandomAccess</code>, <code>Cloneable</code>, <code>Serializable</code>.</li>\n<li>Keeps <strong>insertion order</strong>, allows <strong>duplicates</strong> and <strong>null</strong>, and reads by index in O(1).</li>\n<li><strong>Thread-safe</strong> because each method locks the vector. This makes it slower than ArrayList when only one thread is used.</li>\n<li>Default capacity is <strong>10</strong>. When full it <strong>doubles</strong> its capacity (or grows by a fixed <code>capacityIncrement</code> if you set one).</li>\n<li>Has many <strong>old-style methods</strong> (<code>addElement</code>, <code>elementAt</code>, <code>removeElement</code>, <code>elements()</code>) next to the modern List methods.</li>\n<li><strong>Modern advice:</strong> use <code>ArrayList</code> for single-threaded code, and <code>CopyOnWriteArrayList</code> or <code>Collections.synchronizedList</code> when you need thread safety. Learn Vector mainly to read old code and for interviews.</li>\n</ul>"
