      "content": "<h3>Disadvantages and when NOT to use it</h3>\n<p>For new code, <code>Vector</code> is rarely the best choice. Here is why.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Slower.</strong> Every call takes a lock, even if only one thread is running. A plain <code>ArrayList</code> is faster in that case.</li>\n<li><strong>Doubles in size by default.</strong> When full, a Vector grows to twice its capacity (unless you set an increment), which wastes more memory than ArrayList (1.5 times).</li>\n<li><strong>Old and wordy API.</strong> Methods like <code>addElement</code>, <code>elementAt</code> and <code>removeAllElements</code> duplicate the modern List methods.</li>\n<li><strong>Safe methods, unsafe sequences.</strong> \"Check then act\" (<code>if (!v.contains(x)) v.add(x)</code>) is two calls, and another thread can sneak in between. You still need your own <code>synchronized</code> block.</li>\n<li><strong>Iterators are still fail-fast.</strong> Looping over a Vector while another thread changes it can throw <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> for shared data use <code>CopyOnWriteArrayList</code> (read-heavy), <code>Collections.synchronizedList</code> (when you control the lock), or a concurrent queue. The class is not deprecated but is considered legacy.</li>\n</ul>"
