      "content": "<h3>1. addElement, insertElementAt and add</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>void addElement(E obj);  void insertElementAt(E obj, int index);  boolean add(E e)</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> nothing / nothing / <code>true</code></li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(1) amortised / O(n)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> <code>addElement(x)</code> and <code>add(x)</code> both append at the end. <code>insertElementAt(x, i)</code> puts x at index i and shifts the rest right. Note the <strong>argument order</strong>: element first, then index (the opposite of <code>add(int, E)</code>).</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> <code>addElement</code> is the original method; <code>add</code> was added for the List interface and simply calls the same code. Each call takes the vector's lock, so a loop of a million adds pays for a million lock operations (the JVM can often optimise this away, but the lock is still part of the contract).</p>",
